is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s Linux firewall (iptables) on the WAN/core-facing interface. Router6 sits between the core (10.50.1.0/24) and Location C LANs (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24).

Mechanism: Transit traffic that must cross router6 is being dropped by filter rules on the FORWARD chain. Only a very narrow exception (TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1) is permitted; all other forwarded traffic arriving on eth2 (the core link, 10.50.1.2) is dropped. As a result, pings/flows from Location A to hosts in Location C time out, and replies from Location C back to Location A are also dropped on return (they arrive via eth2 and hit the DROP rule).

Localization: Router6, interface eth2 (10.50.1.2). Evidence:
- From PC4/PC5 (172.16.10.x) to PC15 (172.18.51.15) times out; router0 can ping router6’s interface IPs (172.18.50.254/172.18.51.254), proving reachability to router6 itself.
- Router3→PC15 also times out.
- Router6 iptables: 
  -A FORWARD -s 172.16.10.0/24 -d 172.18.51.1/32 -i eth2 -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP (plus TCP/UDP specific drops)

Root cause: A misconfigured iptables policy on router6 that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, except a single web exception.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the FORWARD chain rules on router6 to allow normal bidirectional forwarding between eth2 and the LANs (e.g., iptables -F FORWARD, or replace with explicit ACCEPT rules for 10.50.1.0/24 <-> 172.18.50.0/24,172.18.51.0/24, including ICMP). Ensure policy/persistence scripts do not reapply the restrictive eth2 DROP rules. Afterward, end-to-end reachability from 172.16.10.0/24 to Location C should be restored. 
=== END ===